  "query_preview": "..Broad high pressure extends from southern\nCalifornia to the Gulf of Mexico and will lead to the hottest \ntemperatures of the year so far. The ridge will stay in place \nthrough most of the work week then move southward as a strong\nand anomalous trough moves into the Pacific Northwest and Great\nBasin this weekend. Today through Friday high temperatures will be \n4-10 degrees above normal with today and Wednesday likely being\nthe warmest days. The Tucson metro could see a streak of five days\nof 105 degree highs or higher, which started yesterday. Some\nareas of the metro could see highs of 110 degrees today into\nThursday with NBM probabilities ranging from 20-50 percent.\nThese warm temperatures will lead to areas of major HeatRisk in\nthe Tucson metro, San Pedro River Valley, and the Gila River \nValley. An Extreme Heat Warning is in effect for these areas \ntoday and Wednesday.\n\nThe placement of the ridge will put southern Arizona under\nsoutherly mid-level flow, which will allow a small amount of \nmoisture to make its way into the region. NBM PoPs have\ndecreased in coverage and chance over the last 24 hours but\nstill a slight chance for storms Wednesday and Thursday over the\ninternational border from Nogales east and the White Mountains. Winds\nwill also be breezy each afternoon in Graham and Greenlee \nCounties into the weekend, becoming breezy area wide Friday into\nearly next week as the trough passes to the north. Winds could\nbe strong enough Saturday and Sunday to produce areas of Red\nFlag conditions and will need to be monitored as the weekend\napproaches.",
